GICv2 per-CPU bank bring-up so the secondary self-IPI clears SIGPdisabled.

0151 proved CPU1 executes our entry (mark=0x7 init=1, affinity 1->2->0) but the
SGI handshake never completes (sig=0 forever).  The GIC-400 banks the SGI/PPI
distributor registers (IGROUPR0/IPRIORITYR0/ICFGR1/ISENABLER0) and the CPU
interface (GICC_CTLR/PMR/BPR) per PE; pe_init_fiq() only ran on the boot CPU,
so CPU1's bank was never programmed and the self-IPI stayed pending.

  pexpert/arm/pe_fiq.c
    - pe_gic_cpu_enable(): program THIS PE's bank (Group 1 for SGIs/PPIs,
      priority 0xA0, ICFGR1 level, ISENABLER0 |= SGI1|vtimer, GICC_PMR=0xFF,
      GICC_BPR=0, GICC_CTLR |= ENABLEGRP1|ENABLEGRP1A) + dsb/isb.  Idempotent.
    - pe_gic_cpu_state(): read back this PE's GICC_CTLR/GICC_PMR/
      GICD_ISENABLER0 (0 if the GIC is not mapped yet).
  iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetCPU.cpp
    - initCPU(): before signalCPU(this) print the before state, call
      pe_gic_cpu_enable(), print the after state
      (`SWEET CPU: cpu=%u GIC self before/after en=.. ctlr=.. pmr=.. isen=..`);
      after queueing print `signalCPU done (SGI%u sig=0x..)`;
    - `sweetsgidbg=1` fallback (default off): clear SIGPdisabled directly after
      the IPI is queued (audit in the plan: only safe as a last-resort
      bring-up hack; it defeats XNU's "IPIs blocked until the startup thread
      unmasks IRQs" serialisation but the queued IPI still runs later);
    - timeout path prints the failing CPU's raw cpu_signal and CpuData VA
      (`sig_raw=0x%x cdp=0x%llx`) to tell "IPI not delivered" from "handler ran
      but did not clear the bit".
  osfmk/arm64/cpu.c
    - SWEET helpers: sweet_cpu_get_signal_raw(), sweet_cpu_data_va(),
      sweet_cpu_sigpdisabled_clear().

Expected: `SWEET CPU: cpu=1 GIC self after en=1 ctlr=0x...3 isen=0x...03`
(bit1 SGI1 + vtimer bit set), then `signalCPU done`, then the boot CPU prints
`came alive; SGI1 OK` and finally `SWEET CPU: 4/4 CPUs initialized`.
